Both endpoints will happily serve unlimited requests. `/users` in particular will be expensive once it hits a real database.

**Recommendation**:

```js
const rateLimit = require('express-rate-limit');
app.use(rateLimit({
  windowMs: 60 * 1000,
  max: 100,
  standardHeaders: true,
  legacyHeaders: false,
}));
```

---

### 🟡 4. No CORS policy defined (OWASP A05)

Without explicit CORS config, browser behavior is "default same-origin" — usually safe, but as soon as someone adds `app.use(cors())` to "fix" a frontend issue, you're wide open. Be explicit now:

```js
const cors = require('cors');
app.use(cors({
  origin: ['https://your-frontend.example.com'],
  methods: ['GET'],
  credentials: false,
}));
```

